    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: when `POST /api/owners` repeats with an already-seen `Idempotency-Key`, return the originally created owner with **200** instead of creating a duplicate.\n\nI implemented this as a single new, self-contained class \u2014 `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/IdempotentCreateFilter.java` \u2014 a Spring `OncePerRequestFilter` (auto-registered as a `@Component`, so it hooks into the request pipeline with **zero edits to any existing method or file**):\n\n- On a POST carrying `Idempotency-Key`, it checks an in-memory store.\n- First time: it buffers the handler's response (`ContentCachingResponseWrapper`), and if the status is 2xx, caches the body + content type under that key before letting it flush normally (still the original 201 Created).\n- Repeat: it replays the cached body with status **200 OK**, never touching the controller \u2014 so no duplicate is created and the same owner id comes back.\n\n**Why this keeps structural impact low:** the cost model punishes editing methods inside large classes (WMC_other pays for the whole class). Touching `addOwner` in the big `OwnerRestControllerV1` would multiply a high-CC method by the summed complexity of ~10 sibling methods. Instead, all new behaviour lives in one small, cohesive class with two trivial methods and near-zero surrounding complexity, and exactly one file is touched.\n\nTests: the new `AcceptanceTest.coreIdempotentRepeat` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
